// Koru Standard Library: the `wireval` part of runtime — the wire grammar's
// teaching prose and its rejection vocabulary. Joined via `~part wireval`
// in runtime.kz; merged into the runtime module at load.
//
// WIRE_GRAMMAR_TEXT and the `wirescan` scanner are the pair that must move
// together: the text describes exactly what the scanner enforces.

const WIRE_GRAMMAR_TEXT =
    \\A wire turn is one flow: verb(field: value, field: value) with `|` arms chaining what each call issues.
    \\- An arm binds what a call issued to a name for the next call: `| ok u |>` for an ordinary result, `| chosen c |>` for a named branch — the arm word is the branch name in the signature.
    \\- The verb is a bare name from your vocabulary — letters, digits, '-', '_' — immediately followed by '('.
    \\- A value is a double-quoted string (escape \" and \\ inside) or a bare literal: a number, true/false, or a name a `|` arm already bound.
    \\- A field typed `Expression` takes expression text bare (`calc(expr: 2 + 3)`); a field typed `Source` takes prose bare (`note(text: thinking this through)`) — and a verb whose only field is Source also answers to `verb: prose`.
    \\- A field typed `name<x>` takes only a bound name the session minted `<x!>` — an `rN` from an earlier verb or an arm binding, never literal text. `name<!x>` spends it.
    \\- A bound name keeps its fields: `r3.user_id` reads that field out of the value r3 bound — the whole result stays readable in the session, not just its name.
    \\- A second invocation starts a second item: one invocation per line runs each in order with one line of report per step.
    \\- Prose is not wire — it binds as residue: "residue bound: rN — your reply was not wire; nothing reached the user" keeps YOUR reply at rN; nothing hears it until a verb takes it as an argument.
;

// `not_invocation` marks the refusals where nothing ever lexed as `verb(` —
// prose, markdown fences, a lone arm — as opposed to a malformed invocation.
// The bridge binds those items as residue so the model's own words become a
// session value it can re-emit through a real verb.
const WireRejection = struct { message: []const u8, byte: usize, not_invocation: bool };

// The character predicates (`wireIdentStart`/`wireIdentChar`/
// `wireBareValueChar`), rejection builders (`wireReject`/`wireRejectResidue`),
// and `wireLineColumn` live in runtime.wirelex.kz (the `wirelex` part). The
// scanner is the `wirescan` part — type-directed there, scope-blind when
// called without lookups; the arms commentary lives with the code.

